The Western Australian Government plans to scrap the Pastoral Lands Board.
The decision comes as a part of efforts by the government to open more diversification opportunities for WA's pastoral rangelands, which cover nearly 90 million hectares.
The Pastoral Lands Board is a statutory authority under the Land Administration Act and has joint responsibility with the Minister for administering pastoral leases.
Together they ensure the leases are managed sustainably, monitor the number and effect of livestock on pastoral land and develop policies to prevent the degradation of the rangelands.
Minister for Regional Development and Lands, Terry Redman, said the PLB will be replaced with the Rangelands Advisory Board.
