Your team, your call, even if I was slightly offended by the bomb reference, but two years on, do you still think it just needs a tweak, Mr. M.?
Let��s review. A few months after suggesting his team required ��a few good bounces,�� the Senators missed the 2009 playoffs by a country mile (10 points), the first post-season flypast by the organization since 1997.
Last season, Ottawa needed a miraculous 11-game winning streak from Jan. 14 to earn a playoff spot before getting bounced in a six-game, first round series against the Pittsburgh Penguins.
That��s two playoff game victories in the past two seasons from a team that reached the Stanley Cup final in 2006-07.
Now, there is again a decent chance Ottawa will miss rather than make the 2010 playoffs, despite laying down a big three-year deal for free-agent defenceman Sergei Gonchar.
See a trend, here?
Judging by his comments during a pre-season roundtable, Melnyk seems to be coming around to the idea of restructuring, suggesting the team would be free of a few burdensome contracts after 2009-10 and would look to fill those spots with homegrown talent rather than with July free agents.
But who will be making those critical decisions?
While the hockey world is buzzing about the future of head coach Cory Clouston after Ottawa��s distressing 5-1 home-ice loss to the Toronto Maple Leafs on Saturday, the bigger question is: What is up at the general manager level?
If it��s true, the rampant rumour that Murray wanted to fire Clouston several weeks ago, but couldn��t get the OK from Melnyk (Murray has already hired three coaches since moving from head coach to GM in 2007), then Murray is a full-fledged lame duck.
Neither Murray or Clouston, brought in to replace Craig Hartsburg in early February 2009, has a contract past this season.
In the twilight of a good, long career, Murray signed on last spring for one more year, clearly hoping to be in position to hand the reins to his nephew, assistant GM Tim Murray, when the time was right.
The timing is lousy, at the moment, with the Senators holding down 10th in the Eastern Conference, eight points out of a playoff spot and no sign of them getting traction.
With centre Jason Spezza likely out for another four to six weeks with a shoulder injury, a ghostly voice seems to echo through the corridors of Scotiabank Place: ��Rebuild … rebuild … ��
But who will make the calls for the future? If it is going to be Murray, he should have an understanding with Melnyk by now that he is sticking around for another year.
If it is not going to be Murray, then Melnyk should be well into his search for a replacement, because it makes no sense to have a lame-duck GM acting on the club��s behalf at the Feb. 28 trade deadline, where the Senators will likely be sellers, not buyers, for the first time in ages.
The GM making those key decisions should be the same one who plans for the upcoming entry draft and free agent period.
