Diana and I didn't say much as we travelled to Switzerland that September day. All the decisions had been made; there was nothing really to talk about. Our minds were totally concentrated on what lay ahead. Diana's cousin and his wife came with us and drove us up to Heathrow for the flight to Zurich.
My daughter and her fiance came to the airport to say goodbye and then off we went. Club class, I seem to remember. Four seats out, three seats back. It did seem a bit odd. We were on holiday in Italy in the late 1990s when Diana became aware something wasn't quite right with her leg. Eventually she got diagnosed with motor neurone disease.
I didn't know what the hell that was. I didn't realise how awful it was going to be. I thought: 'Oh well, we'll probably be able to sort this out.' But of course they weren't able to sort it out. It's a vile, horrible, wasting disease that steadily takes away all your functions.
One day we were watching a documentary about someone going to Zurich and having assisted suicide, and Diana said, 'That's what I want to do!' I was rather astounded, quite astonished. It's a rather unusual thing for your spouse to say. But I was fully supportive. The thing that struck me was we'd had two or three dogs who we'd had put down and I thought if it's right to have a dog put down because it's suffering, why isn't it right for a human being?
There's a hell of a lot of hypocrisy talked about the sanctity of human life. By the time we arranged for her to go to Switzerland the disease had really advanced. She was in a wheelchair a lot of the time and had to be helped into bed; it was very humiliating. She hadn't been able to go to the loo by herself for two years – imagine what that's like. It really is awful, horrible to see it. You can't really go on. It was just a miserable existence for her, miserable, and she only had a year or two more.
But once the decision had been made and it had been agreed it was OK, she perked up quite a bit. We used to have quite a jolly time in a way, in those two or three months. Not exactly parties, but saying goodbye to people. People would come along, bring a bottle of champagne. Of course it was emotional, but it was really nice for her to be able to do that.
I never worried about being prosecuted, I didn't give a damn. I thought what the hell, if they want to prosecute me I'm not going to be going to jail for very long anyway. When we got to Zurich someone from the clinic met us. We'd taken the wheelchair with us. At Dignitas they've got a great stock of one-way wheelchairs that I assume people have left. We added ours to the pile. I thought, I'm not going to go back to England with an empty wheelchair, it's too bloody ridiculous.
