Linnet Wilkins Jordan of Hampton recalls the many winters of snow she went through when she lived in New York City.
Jordan retired nineyears ago after 30 years of service as a supervisor of switchboard operators at Holmes Protective Association, a New York security firm that protects residences, banks and other commercial firms.
"We were affiliated with the New York Telephone Co.," she says. "It was a pretty good job. I came to Hampton six years."
Jordan is not new to the area. She was born and raised in Suffolk, where she was a graduate from Booker T. Washington High School. She earned a bachelor of science degree from St. Paul's College, Lawerenceville, where she majored in elementary education. She was a teacher for five years in Powhatan County public schools.
She enjoyed teaching because she always liked working with children, she says.
"But, after I was married, I moved to New York to be with my husband, Richard, who was a clerk with Lewmac Co., a shipping firm," she says.
She was a member of the New York Calvary Baptist Church for 25 years where she taught children about Christianity. She continues to teach children at her church in Hampton.
In addition, under the sponsorship and the Retired Seniors Volunteer Program, she has volunteered to teach people to read and write for about a year.
Currently, she is tutoring John Shaw, 28, of Hampton, a sickle cell anemia victim who never had the opportunity to learn how to read and write, she says.
