Cook County's beleaguered juvenile detention center has agreed to rehire five counselors who were fired for allegedly physically abusing residents, but they won't have any contact with children in their new jobs.
Taxpayers will be on the hook for $261,000 a year in salaries for the workers, who were accused of hitting, kicking, punching and slamming down youths. The rub, however, doesn't end there: Because the rehired employees are no longer allowed to work with children, the detention center has to hire five new counselors to work with the 400 youths accused of crimes.
In three cases, the detention center's previous leadership bungled the firings by failing to present proper evidence, an arbitrator ruled.
Earl Dunlap, appointed by a federal judge as transitional administrator to improve conditions at the long-troubled center, said provisions in a union contract and the dismissal of the cases on appeal leave him no course but to rehire the workers for administrative jobs.
"I'm not at all promoting these people coming back to work, but sometimes you have to do what you have to do," he said. "The law says you have to do it."
Though county commissioners say allegations against the employees were overturned by technicalities, an attorney representing the workers through Teamsters Local 714 said the five men deserve their jobs back.
"The union is satisfied that if the settlement is achieved, that the rights of its members have been protected and recognized by all of the parties," attorney Robert Bloch said.
The County Board is being asked to approve rehiring the counselors fired over abuse allegations and hiring their replacements. Commissioner Earlean Collins (D-Chicago), chairwoman of the Criminal Justice Committee, said she will not sponsor the amendment. "No one else will," Collins said.
Three of the employees were accused of a range of offenses, including unspecified abuse and hitting a resident on the back of the head, then kicking him several times in the chest, sources said.
Detention center officials fired all three, relying on findings by the Illinois Department of Children and Family Services.
But an arbitrator determined the three employees -- all dismissed nearly nine months before Dunlap was appointed in August 2007 -- were improperly fired. The ruling said the county failed to produce adequate evidence that child abuse had occurred, as required under the Teamsters contract.
A fourth employee, fired two months before Dunlap was brought in, allegedly punched a resident. DCFS again found credible evidence that abuse had occurred, but an administrative law judge overturned that finding on appeal.
The fifth employee, a recreational staffer Dunlap dismissed under his "zero tolerance" policy, allegedly admitted yanking a youth to the floor by his legs to defuse a disruptive situation instead of using a restraining technique as policy required. A hearing officer determined the employee should have been suspended but not fired.
Dunlap initially refused to rehire any of the employees, but the Teamsters took the case to federal court.
A settlement conference last Friday led to a tentative compromise in which the employees would be rehired, provided they did not work with children. A final deal could be approved by U.S. District Chief Judge James Holderman as early as this week.
"They will come back to work one way or another because of the law," Dunlap said. "It's a question of how they are coming back to work.... My bottom line is they should not be working with kids."
The American Civil Liberties Union, which has been battling for a decade with the county about violent and unsanitary conditions in the detention center, supports Dunlap's position.
"We have complained about staff abusing the youth at the detention center for many years," said Benjamin Wolf, an attorney with the group.
